wolf test — discover and run `*_test.lu` files.

usage: wolf test [<dir>|<file.lu>]… [--std-root <dir>] [--deny-warnings]
            [--error-limit=N] [--json] [--nocapture] [--no-native-fallback]

No registration and no life-before-main: a file whose name ends
`_test.lu` is a test file, and each of its top-level zero-parameter
`fn test_*` is one test, run in declaration order in a fresh machine. A
test file with no `test_*` but a `main` runs black-box as one test.

A row is `ok`, `FAILED` (it ran and found something), `REJECTED` (the
compiler rejected the file, so it never ran) or `unsupported` (the
checked machine refused a construct). Exit codes: 0 every test passed,
1 any row was not `ok`, 2 a usage or environment error.

Tests run on the checked machine. A `main` test it refuses (a budget, a
construct outside its surface) runs once natively instead, and its row
says so: `ok [native: step budget exhausted]`, counted in the summary
(`N passed, M of them native`) and still a pass; `--no-native-fallback`
reports it `unsupported`. `--nocapture` shows each test's stdout and
stderr as it writes them (both on stderr under `--json`). Under `wolf
test` the checked machine refuses `os_exe()` by name, so a test that
re-spawns itself runs natively, where `os_exe()` is its own binary.
wolf conform-run — observe one program and emit a conformance record.

usage: wolf conform-run <file.lu> [--phase=<p>] [--seed=N] [--json]
            [--error-format=human|json] [--dump=regions|cfg|wir] [--zstats]
            [--checked] [--native] [--release] [--std-root <dir>]
            [--deny-warnings]

The differential-testing entry point: stdout carries one observation
record in the specification's format, stderr the diagnostics. This is
how the compiler and the reference interpreter are compared against
each other on the same program. Not a verb you need in order to write
wolf.

`--deny-warnings` promotes every warning to an error, so a program
that warns is `fail(CODE)` in the record — the gate a consumer coupled
to this surface alone could not otherwise reach. No other lint flag is
read here, and the manifest's `lints.*` rules are not read either: a
record has to be reproducible from the file and the command line.
